'''Habax Balboa, SC''' ({{lang|ca|Habax Balboa, Societat Cooperativa}}) is a Balboan worker cooperative operating a chain of specialty coffeehouses and a coffee roasting operation in the principal Balboan metropolitan areas. The cooperative is the Balboan offshoot of the [[Habax Coffee Cooperative]] of [[Cabu Verd]], [[Balisca]], and imports green coffee from the Baliscan parent cooperative, which it roasts and serves in the Sanese kissaten tradition established by the [[Kashiwagi family]].

Habax Balboa is owned by its workers, who hold equal shares and govern the enterprise through a general assembly. The cooperative is a member of the [[Balboan Cooperative Federation]].

== History ==

=== Origins ===

The Habax Coffee Cooperative of Cabu Verd, Balisca, was founded in 1961 by [[Kashiwagi Renji]], a Sanese immigrant from [[Toyozawa]], [[Okaiken]], who applied the kissaten coffee roasting traditions of his homeland to the arabica beans grown on the volcanic slopes of the Cabu Verd archipelago. The cooperative developed a distinctive specialty coffee — light-roasted, bright, and floral — that gained recognition in Okaiken's specialty kissaten market and, under the [[Habax Framework]] signed in 2025, received preferential trade status in Okaiken.

The extension of the Habax brand to Balboa was initiated by [[Kashiwagi Mika]], the cooperative's third-generation master roaster and cultural liaison, who identified Balboa as an underserved market for specialty coffee. Balboa's coffee culture was dominated by the Volisanian-style espresso bars and the interior's traditional café culture, but the Sanese kissaten model — slow, pour-over preparation, emphasis on single-origin beans, and the coffeehouse as a space for quiet contemplation — had no presence in the country.

Kashiwagi Mika approached the [[Balboan Cooperative Federation]] in 2018, proposing a Balboan cooperative that would import green coffee from the Cabu Verd cooperative, roast it locally using the Kashiwagi family's techniques, and operate coffeehouses in the kissaten style. The Balboan Cooperative Federation, which had been seeking to expand the cooperative sector into the urban services economy, supported the proposal and provided organisational assistance.

=== Founding and expansion (2019–2026) ===

Habax Balboa was founded in 2019 with eight founding worker-members, all of whom travelled to Cabu Verd for a training programme under Kashiwagi Mika's supervision. The cooperative opened its first coffeehouse on the [[Plaça Major (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Plaça Major]] in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], in a converted space that reflected the aesthetic of the original kissaten — minimalist, wood-panelled, with a pour-over bar as the focal point.

The first coffeehouse was successful, attracting a clientele of young professionals, students, and the Zong-Balboan community, who recognised the Sanese coffee tradition. The cooperative opened a second location in Portnou in 2020 and a third in Sant Llàtzer in 2021. The [[Adonian pandemic]] slowed the expansion but did not halt it, as the coffeehouses adapted to takeaway and delivery service — partnering with [[ECafè]] for last-mile delivery.

By 2026, Habax Balboa operated 11 coffeehouses across four metropolitan areas — Sant Cristòfor (5), Portnou (3), Sant Llàtzer (2), and Sant Bart (1) — and employed approximately 340 worker-members. The cooperative's roasting operation, located in a small facility in [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]], supplies all of the coffeehouses with freshly roasted beans.

== Cooperative structure ==

Habax Balboa operates as a worker cooperative under Balboan cooperative law. The cooperative's structure is modelled on the Baliscan cooperative tradition established by the parent Habax Coffee Cooperative, adapted to the Balboan legal framework.

All employees who have completed a six-month probationary period are eligible for membership in the cooperative. Each member holds one share and has one vote in the general assembly, regardless of tenure or position. The general assembly elects a cooperative coordinator — the equivalent of a CEO — who manages day-to-day operations, and a roasting committee that oversees quality standards.

The cooperative's relationship with the parent Habax Coffee Cooperative in Cabu Verd is defined by a supply agreement: Habax Balboa purchases green coffee from the Baliscan cooperative at fair-trade prices, and the Baliscan cooperative provides training, quality certification, and brand licensing. The Balboan cooperative is financially independent and does not hold equity in the Baliscan cooperative, nor does the Baliscan cooperative hold equity in the Balboan offshoot.

== Coffee and operations ==

Habax Balboa serves coffee prepared in the Sanese kissaten tradition, using single-origin arabica beans imported from the Cabu Verd cooperative. The coffeehouses offer pour-over, siphon, and espresso preparations, with an emphasis on the pour-over method that highlights the beans' bright acidity, floral notes, and citrus characteristics.

The cooperative's roasting operation, overseen by Kashiwagi Mika through periodic visits and remote quality consultation, follows the Kashiwagi family's light-roast technique. The green beans are roasted in small batches — typically 5 to 10 kilograms — to ensure precise control over the roast profile. The roasted coffee is distributed to the coffeehouses within 48 hours of roasting.

The coffeehouses also serve a selection of Balboan pastries — including traditional [[Balboan cuisine|estret pastries]] and Birú baked goods — sourced from local producers, and a small menu of Sanese-inspired items that reflect the cooperative's cultural heritage.

